data.content:
Here's your test rewritten with modern MSTest collection assertions:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    Assert.IsNotEmpty(users);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));
    Assert.HasCount(3, users);
    Assert.ContainsSingle(users.Where(u => u.Role == "Admin"));
}
```

**What changed:**

| Before | After | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0)` | `Assert.IsNotEmpty(users)` | Clearer intent, better failure message |
| `Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3)` | `Assert.HasCount(3, users)` | Shows expected vs actual count on failure |
| `Single()` + `Assert.IsTrue(x != null)` | `Assert.ContainsSingle(filtered)` | Asserts exactly one match exists; returns it if you need it |

The `users.All(u => u.IsActive)` line is fine as `Assert.IsTrue` — there's no built-in "assert all match predicate" API, so that's the appropriate choice there.

> **Tip:** `Assert.ContainsSingle` also returns the element, so if you need to inspect the admin further:
> ```csharp
> var admin = Assert.ContainsSingle(users.Where(u => u.Role == "Admin"));
> Assert.AreEqual("expected@email.com", admin.Email);
> ```

These assertions require **MSTest 3.8+** (via `MSTest` metapackage or `MSTest.Sdk`).
